lum in a way  that's not overtly political does make  sense in American public schools in a  lot of classes we just don't need  politics in Nath class for example there  are areas though once you get into  science once you get into Human  Geography there are political realities  that are either implicit or unavoidable  in those there  and I still think in those cases  teachers should be able to keep the  advocacy to a minimum don't advocate  necessarily for particular candidates  for example but in science class you've  got to talk about the environment how do  you not talk about climate change  III don't believe climate change is  political it's just the reality but  there are people who do but teachers  have to be able to talk about that it's  just the world around us that they're  describing in college it gets different  I think but more call more more college  politics in the classroom is okay  advocating for or against specific  candidates maybe it should be kept out  of the classroom but we're talking about  adults we're talking about people who  are better able to identify what's a  personal view what is coursework there's  no question that the underlying  philosophy and subject matter in a lot  of college classes is inherently  political and pretending that it can  just be kept out is not reasonable and  you're actually starting to do damage to  students if you artificially keep  politics out so as with most things the  answer is it depends it depends what  we're talking about  it depends what age we're talking about  but some political realities in the  classroom are not only unavoidable they  should be a advisable they're actually  advisable so that people students have a  realistic understanding of what is going  on in the world
 

